bolic disorder. Overall, GSDI presents a unique set of challenges and management strategies for affected individuals.\",15\r\n9470,ALMIDÓN CRUDO DE MAÍZ,\"Almidón crudo de maíz, or raw corn starch, is a dietary supplement specifically recommended for patients with glycogen storage diseases (GSD), including conditions such as GSDI and GSDIX. It plays a crucial role in managing hypoglycemia by providing a steady release of glucose, which is essential for maintaining normal blood sugar levels. This supplement is particularly beneficial for individuals with McArdle disease, as it helps sustain glucose levels before sleeping, and for those with fructose-1,6-bisphosphate deficiency, aiding in their dietary management and prevention of hypoglycemia. Overall, almidón crudo de maíz is an important dietary component that supports patients in maintaining stable blood glucose levels, especially between meals and during periods of fasting.\",8\r\n9485,GSDIII,\"Glycogen Storage Disease Type III (GSDIII), also known as Cori disease, is a metabolic disorder characterized by a deficiency of the enzyme amilo-1,6-glucosidase. This deficiency disrupts normal glycogen metabolism, resulting in various complications that primarily affect glucose processing. Individuals with GSDIII may experience muscular and hepatic issues as a result of this disorder. To manage symptoms and complications associated with GSDIII, dietary interventions are recommended, specifically hyperproteic and low carbohydrate diets.\",23\r\n6120,HEPATOMEGALIA,\"Hepatomegalia is a clinical condition characterized by the enlargement of the liver. This condition can manifest as a result of various metabolic disorders, including a deficiency in lipoprotein lipase. It is commonly associated with glycogen storage diseases (GSD), particularly GSDIII, and can be observed in children suffering from these conditions. Additionally, hepatomegalia may also be a symptom of Wilson's disease, highlighting its relevance in a range of hepatic disorders. Overall, hepatomegalia serves as an important clinical indicator of underlying metabolic issues affecting liver function.\",5\r\n5743,HEPATOCARCINOMA,\"Hepatocarcinoma is a type of liver cancer characterized by its association with various factors, including dietary influences, particularly the consumption of vegetables. It is noted to occur in less than 10% of patients with Glycogen Storage Disease Type III (GSDIII), making it a relatively rare complication in this population. Hepatocarcinoma is recognized as the most common epithelial tumor that can lead to hypoglycemia, primarily due to the overproduction of Insulin-like Growth Factor 2 (IGF-2). Additionally, hepatocellular carcinoma, which is another form of liver cancer, can also manifest in patients with GSDIII, although it is considered rare.\",3\r\n9488,FIBROSIS HEPÁTICA,\"Fibrosis hepática is characterized by the formation of excess fibrous connective tissue in the liver, often resulting from chronic liver diseases, such as hereditary hemochromatosis. This condition involves the scarring of liver tissue, which can occur in various patients, including those with Glycogen Storage Disease type III (GSDIII), although it is uncommon for these patients to progress to cirrhosis.\",2\r\n9493,AFECTACIÓN RENAL,\"Renal involvement, a potential complication in patients with GSDIII, though it is also infrequent.\",1\r\n9486,\"AMILO-1,6-GLUCOSIDASA\",\"Amilo-1,6-glucosidasa is an enzyme responsible for glycogen breakdown, and its deficiency leads to GSDIII.\",1\r\n9487,GEN AGL,\"The AGL gene located on chromosome 1p21.2 encodes the enzyme amilo-1,6-glucosidasa, and mutations in this gene are responsible for GSDIII.\",1\r\n9490,MIKARDIOPATÍA HIPERTRÓFICA,\"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a condition affecting the heart muscle, prevalent in GSDIII patients.\",1\r\n9489,HIPERTONÍA,\"A condition characterized by increased muscle tone, often associated with GSDIII.\",1\r\n9492,HIPOURICEMIA,\"A condition of ele